WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.840
RC Pro-Am, Nintendo puts you in control.

00:00:18.820 --> 00:00:28.040
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:28.540 --> 00:00:29.840
I'm Mike. I'm Joe.

00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:31.420
I'm Sean. And I'm Sam.

00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:33.240
Guys, how are we doing? I'm pretty good.

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:35.560
How are you? You seem very relaxed tonight, Sean.

00:00:35.560 --> 00:00:36.760
I'm a relaxed boy.

00:00:37.060 --> 00:00:40.320
I say tonight, but we always upload the episodes at 8am every Friday.

00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:47.120
If you're one of our first listeners, if you're that guy who gets it at like 8.04, just know that we set them to deliver at 8am.

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:54.740
Theoretically, I could set them to deliver at like 7.56 in the morning, so they get directly to you at 8am, but it's actually a really good idea.

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:56.080
I might start doing that. Yeah, really.

00:00:56.220 --> 00:00:57.280
I don't know why you haven't done that yet.

00:00:57.380 --> 00:00:59.200
But then I'd hate to see it come in at like 8.01.

00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:01.300
What about 7.59?

00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:02.720
Would you be upset about a 7.59?

00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:05.740
7.59 is better than 8.01, because that's early.

00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:07.120
Better early than late.

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:09.480
That's true. So what was everyone's favorite part of this episode?

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:11.120
Was it when we talked about the time?

00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:12.260
It's the only time so far.

00:01:12.980 --> 00:01:14.940
Anyway, let's talk about RC Pro-Am.

00:01:15.020 --> 00:01:16.640
We got another rare game here.

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:18.860
Not that it's rare in its...

00:01:18.860 --> 00:01:21.620
Well, actually, it is pretty rare, because we don't have any racing games yet, right?

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:27.740
We've had like some things that have tried to be racing games, but don't quite get there, right?

00:01:27.860 --> 00:01:29.820
We had Rad Racer.

00:01:30.220 --> 00:01:31.540
Not really a racing game.

00:01:31.540 --> 00:01:33.820
They're all sort of just go fast.

00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:36.360
And go far. What about Excitebike?

00:01:36.440 --> 00:01:38.020
There's Race Against Time also.

00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:39.200
Oh, Excitebike, yeah.

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:40.860
That was a racing game, right?

00:01:40.940 --> 00:01:42.780
You're racing. I feel like a lot of them are racing against time.

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:45.400
Yeah. Or racing against society.

00:01:46.360 --> 00:01:48.180
Mock Rider. Yeah.

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:53.180
Yeah, and even Excitebike, I think, is still like a race against time thing for the most part.

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:57.200
Slalom. Because you have to get it in a certain time to qualify for the next round.

00:01:57.300 --> 00:01:59.020
You don't necessarily have to finish first, I think.

00:01:59.020 --> 00:02:08.700
Whereas this is a game in which your opponents are all at like, you know, a discrete point in the lap.

00:02:09.220 --> 00:02:10.840
Yeah. Like on the course.

00:02:11.180 --> 00:02:18.380
Whereas before, they would just sort of spawn opponents and it was just like, here, just go fast.

00:02:18.860 --> 00:02:25.520
Yeah. So, for those of you that don't know, in RC Pro-Am, you are guiding a remote-controlled car.

00:02:25.780 --> 00:02:26.740
That's the RC.

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:29.120
Yes. It stands for remote control.

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:37.620
And you're guiding this remote-controlled car through like a various amount of stages, but they all look kind of the same for the most part.

00:02:37.940 --> 00:02:41.480
Yeah. And you have to avoid like water and oil puddles.

00:02:41.480 --> 00:02:43.580
You can collect some bonus items.

00:02:43.820 --> 00:02:46.840
You get a trophy. Yeah, you can get better engines and tires.

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:52.820
And you're doing this against three other opponents, which are just the yellow car, the blue car, and the green car.

00:02:52.820 --> 00:02:54.000
You're the red car.

00:02:54.180 --> 00:02:55.260
So that's pretty cool. Can't pick.

00:02:55.500 --> 00:02:56.580
Yeah. That's hot. Yeah.

00:02:56.720 --> 00:02:58.040
But red's like the fast one, right?

00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:00.540
Isn't that why the insurance is so much money on a red car?

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:02.680
Yeah. Because they know people are speed.

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:03.800
Red paint makes you go faster.

00:03:03.800 --> 00:03:05.160
Like candy apple red. That's science.

00:03:05.180 --> 00:03:06.200
I'm sorry. What were you saying, John?

00:03:06.820 --> 00:03:07.720
Candy apple red.

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:09.660
Oh, yes. Also, apples are fast.

00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:12.520
That car from Cars, who's the star of Cars.

00:03:12.520 --> 00:03:14.440
He's red. He says, I am speed.

00:03:14.660 --> 00:03:15.580
And he's red. Right.

00:03:15.580 --> 00:03:17.340
You're right. So by the transitive property.

00:03:17.780 --> 00:03:20.240
Yes, exactly. Although that didn't come out until after this.

00:03:20.980 --> 00:03:22.460
Yes, that's true. Little known fact.

00:03:22.580 --> 00:03:30.960
We're getting a little off topic, though, because even though I've accurately described the game in my own opinion, Sean, there is an element to this game that I haven't talked about yet.

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:32.720
And that is the way that it looks.

00:03:32.720 --> 00:03:35.140
Yeah, it has a very interesting perspective.

00:03:35.560 --> 00:03:42.880
It's at sort of an isometric, like three quarter view that you see more in like CRPGs.

00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:47.960
And it can be really weird when you're trying to control a car.

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:51.480
It was like tank controls out of like Resident Evil.

00:03:51.740 --> 00:03:55.240
Yeah, this is kind of like an out of body experience of driving a car.

00:03:55.240 --> 00:04:06.160
It's like when you got in the car, you were only given the view of instead of like, you know, through your own eyeballs, you were given it from like a GPS satellite and you had to try to still drive the same way.

00:04:06.260 --> 00:04:11.280
It's from the it's basically from the Google Maps 3D view, but not Google Earth.

00:04:11.420 --> 00:04:14.600
You know what I mean? I have played racing games with this perspective before, though.

00:04:14.740 --> 00:04:15.760
Yeah. And I guess it makes sense.

00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:17.000
And it only dawned on me right now.

00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:19.400
It makes sense in the context of a remote control car.

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:21.880
You're not in the car. You're not looking at it from behind the car.

00:04:22.180 --> 00:04:25.680
Absolutely. I hadn't actually was wondering until right now why it's like that.

00:04:25.880 --> 00:04:27.120
Well, I think it's like that for twofold.

00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:31.320
I think the remote control thing is great and a good like easy way for them to get away with it.

00:04:31.400 --> 00:04:39.640
But I also think this was a design choice because then they can keep they can show placement of all the other cars as well on the map.

00:04:39.780 --> 00:04:49.500
So this way, if you were only doing it like how Rad Racer did it, where you saw like the top of your car and it was more like a straight on point of view.

00:04:49.500 --> 00:04:55.780
You never knew where anybody else was on the track besides maybe a few frames in front of you if they happened to draw their car.

00:04:55.940 --> 00:04:57.320
And combat is important in this.

00:04:57.460 --> 00:05:11.280
Yeah, exactly. So I think here like spatial awareness is also very important to know like, OK, I'm in first, but I can see the guy in second behind me and he might like try to push me off the track or shoot in a weapon.

00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:24.640
Yeah, it's also to me the easiest way to give a faux 3D look to any game because it has that depth because it's at that weird angle.

00:05:25.280 --> 00:05:30.700
And it just looks better than what we've seen from like the first person view so far.

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:36.100
Would you say that this even, you know, put your 1988 goggles on.

00:05:36.300 --> 00:05:40.040
Is this a visually impressive game on the NES so far?

00:05:40.040 --> 00:05:40.860
I think so.

00:05:41.140 --> 00:05:44.380
OK, no, I guess that's what I'm asking is open it up to the room, too.

00:05:44.820 --> 00:05:48.780
I didn't load it and think like, wow, look at these graphics compared to other games.

00:05:48.880 --> 00:05:52.800
I think we've also been kind of spoiled having been able to play every game.

00:05:53.000 --> 00:05:59.600
Yeah, I think the aesthetic choices are a little like bland, like generic looking.

00:05:59.820 --> 00:06:05.320
But in terms of how they present the information, it's very clear and it holds up.

00:06:05.420 --> 00:06:06.240
It holds up well.

00:06:06.240 --> 00:06:14.240
Yeah, I think to me it falls kind of firmly and like for the time doing just what it needs to do to kind of get by and like not look bad.

00:06:14.380 --> 00:06:15.920
Doesn't look great.

00:06:16.060 --> 00:06:17.720
It didn't blow me away, but it looks fine.

00:06:17.840 --> 00:06:19.180
It looks passable.

00:06:19.360 --> 00:06:20.860
OK, cool. I think it looks good.

00:06:21.060 --> 00:06:30.640
Yeah. And so let's get into controls of the car specifically and how that works in this isometric point of view, but also on various different tracks.

00:06:30.820 --> 00:06:33.980
I don't want to talk about the tune up items or weapons or anything like that.

00:06:34.160 --> 00:06:40.660
I just want to talk about how it felt to steer your car in this viewpoint and like if that was really the best way to go about it.

00:06:40.700 --> 00:06:41.640
So who wants to take that?

00:06:41.780 --> 00:06:42.720
I can take it. Cool.

00:06:42.720 --> 00:06:46.520
I think that it was a good viewpoint and everything works well with the steering.

00:06:46.900 --> 00:06:53.080
I think so. When you are going into a turn and you're accelerating, you kind of skid a little bit too, which I think is cool.

00:06:53.180 --> 00:06:58.000
And if you don't accelerate, you take your hand off the, you know, the A button, I think.

00:06:59.660 --> 00:07:03.300
Then you can kind of you can do a lot sharper turns, I found.

00:07:04.180 --> 00:07:06.220
So I think that the controls are pretty tight.

00:07:06.420 --> 00:07:09.400
They were really sensitive, which it says in the in the manual.

00:07:09.760 --> 00:07:19.020
But I think that that's something that you can get used to and then start, you know, memorizing tracks and getting really like, you know, just getting really intricate with it.

00:07:19.180 --> 00:07:23.500
Yeah, there's definitely a learning curve to the to the how delicate the controls are.

00:07:23.500 --> 00:07:32.520
And also, I mean, just me personally, I always have a hard time when when you're doing this camera kind of stays facing the same direction, but you have to turn and drive back towards yourself.

00:07:33.020 --> 00:07:35.820
Because then I start to get the turning backwards at first.

00:07:35.900 --> 00:07:38.140
So it's a little bit of learning curve on that element.

00:07:38.220 --> 00:07:43.980
But once you kind of get the hang of it all, I think it's like really fast pace and like fun to drive these cars.

00:07:44.280 --> 00:07:53.840
It feels good. And we've seen this mechanic of like being prepared to slow down and turn in games like Mach Rider, where you, you know, all of a sudden you'll know a turn is coming up.

00:07:53.900 --> 00:08:01.140
But if you go too fast and don't prepare for the turn ahead of time, you'll be hanging out on the edge of the track the entire time.

00:08:01.220 --> 00:08:05.620
Now, in Mach Rider, you still move forward progression because of the way that it worked here.

00:08:05.800 --> 00:08:06.600
You wouldn't go anywhere.

00:08:06.760 --> 00:08:09.080
You would just go off the course and hit the wall.

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:10.080
And you can crash that way.

00:08:10.200 --> 00:08:21.420
Yeah. So in this regard, I think that the way that they've communicated like being delicate about the controls is that if you aren't delicate, you will think that you suck at this game.

00:08:21.940 --> 00:08:26.200
Like it's a very, it's slow to learn, at least for me.

00:08:26.420 --> 00:08:28.100
I didn't love the controls right away.

00:08:28.520 --> 00:08:35.500
But because it's so punishing, if you don't take it easy, you're forced to play that way and you do get better as a result.

00:08:35.500 --> 00:08:48.140
I think this is the kind of game that even on the very first track, if you're not controlling your car the proper way and you're just gunning for like a first place finish and, you know, like, oh, I'm supposed to win every time no matter what.

00:08:48.260 --> 00:08:49.500
That's not going to work out here.

00:08:49.500 --> 00:09:01.820
I would argue, though, that maybe some of your struggles that you're attributing to the controls and the speed of the game may have more to do with the amount of the track that you see at once.

00:09:01.820 --> 00:09:10.860
Because it's really, I mean, like they do give you on the track, they'll have arrows pointing in the direction you'll be turning next as a warning.

00:09:11.100 --> 00:09:20.560
But you only see like a small sliver of the track at one time and a very small amount of room behind and in front of you and, you know, to the top of you and below you.

00:09:21.380 --> 00:09:27.740
So you have to kind of memorize the track before you can get really elegant with your turns.

00:09:27.880 --> 00:09:30.340
You have to just react at first.

00:09:30.340 --> 00:09:32.480
And I think that's where a lot of struggles come from.

00:09:33.160 --> 00:09:45.820
Yeah. But that being said, I think that in some of the later races when there's more than one lap, I felt I really felt like I could catch up at the end of it because I had memorized the track after, you know, after two laps of going around.

00:09:45.820 --> 00:09:52.180
And they're all short tracks and you do get that overhead monitor in the UI below you.

00:09:52.380 --> 00:09:57.480
But your point, Sean, was going to be my next point about just how much of the screen they show.

00:09:57.660 --> 00:10:02.700
And I'm sure that's a graphical limitation thing, a processing power thing.

00:10:02.960 --> 00:10:09.840
But for me, I agree that I never quite like overcame that like, oh, the arrows say to go here.

00:10:09.960 --> 00:10:13.800
So I'll know exactly where to turn on my D-pad when that shows up.

00:10:13.800 --> 00:10:19.040
It was still kind of like, you know, an instantaneous reaction until you learn the course.

00:10:19.040 --> 00:10:21.900
The problem being that there are 32 tracks.

00:10:22.300 --> 00:10:28.880
So, you know, you can't you can learn like a few of them at a time as you continue to play the game often.

00:10:29.420 --> 00:10:38.040
But unless you have an innate memory ability to like be, you know, like picture that stuff in your head, you're not going to remember all 32 of these tracks.

00:10:38.040 --> 00:10:44.260
More often for me, I was I was remembering specific turns by like the third or fourth sometimes lap of that race.

00:10:44.420 --> 00:10:48.300
But never like coming back to that track, remembering it from the last time I had that race.

00:10:48.300 --> 00:10:51.000
It was just second, third, fourth lap.

00:10:51.140 --> 00:10:53.260
I was like, oh, yeah, this is where that big sharp turn is.

00:10:53.380 --> 00:10:56.000
And I don't I just I want to be sure.

00:10:56.220 --> 00:10:58.280
I don't think I'm completely correct here.

00:10:58.280 --> 00:11:03.940
But I don't think that there are that many permutations of the track layout itself.

00:11:03.940 --> 00:11:07.740
I think that the later levels just add more obstacles.

00:11:08.620 --> 00:11:14.460
Yeah, I think that even though it says 32, even on the back of the box, I still believe it's like 24.

00:11:14.640 --> 00:11:16.680
Oh, unique track layout.

00:11:16.900 --> 00:11:20.680
And then like as soon as that ends, then you kind of just like remix.

00:11:21.020 --> 00:11:26.580
Yeah. Remixes slash like just straight copies, but maybe in no particular order.

00:11:26.880 --> 00:11:32.620
Interesting. OK. Yeah.

00:11:33.000 --> 00:11:36.320
So I think that's a good overview of the car controls and stuff like that.

00:11:36.500 --> 00:11:40.940
Now let's let's add the AI element to it and have the other cars.

00:11:41.920 --> 00:11:43.680
What's the difficulty like in the game?

00:11:43.760 --> 00:11:46.520
How hard is it to finish in first place in a game like this?

00:11:46.560 --> 00:11:47.540
I felt like it was fair.

00:11:47.760 --> 00:11:55.500
I think that if you play the game the right way, if you play each race the right way, then you can win.

00:11:55.500 --> 00:11:58.620
And not easily, but you can finish.

00:11:58.620 --> 00:12:00.120
I mean, fairly easily.

00:12:00.120 --> 00:12:10.780
And to speak to something that you said earlier, Sam, is that I really felt also that there were a lot of times I was really far behind, but I very rarely felt like I was out of the race.

00:12:10.780 --> 00:12:12.760
Like I always felt like, OK, it's still possible.

00:12:12.900 --> 00:12:17.900
I can catch up. And there were a few times where I was worried I was out of the race, but just kept playing just to play.

00:12:18.060 --> 00:12:20.480
And then I was like, oh, they're kind of not that far away from me.

00:12:20.540 --> 00:12:21.500
Like I can still do this.

00:12:21.500 --> 00:12:26.700
So they always felt, not always, but frequently felt attainable.

00:12:26.920 --> 00:12:40.780
Yeah, I don't know the nuts and bolts of the game, but I have to imagine that there's some rubber banding going in there, whether you're trailing or the other computer players are trailing.

00:12:40.780 --> 00:12:44.240
Because there is a lot of quick catching up, I noticed.

00:12:46.180 --> 00:12:56.460
But I thought that the amount of chaos that the game brings into it, like any kart racer, it was fun regardless of how well I was performing.

00:12:57.040 --> 00:13:00.480
Right. And sometimes that's the whole appeal of it, right?

00:13:00.640 --> 00:13:04.160
It are the obstacles in the courses and the weapons, too.

00:13:04.160 --> 00:13:14.480
Like I think the fact that this has both obstacles and weapons in it from the beginning and understanding that you need those things in a kart racer style game is important to have here.

00:13:14.700 --> 00:13:17.980
Without either one of those, it would feel kind of empty.

00:13:18.200 --> 00:13:21.180
You know, if it was just guiding through the tracks.

00:13:21.400 --> 00:13:29.220
Yeah. I think, you know, even someone like me who had difficulty navigating the tracks would still find it very boring if there wasn't like, oh, you got to watch out for that oil spill.

00:13:29.220 --> 00:13:36.520
Or, you know, or like, oh, I can get in front of the blue guy if I just shoot the missile right at him.

00:13:36.660 --> 00:13:43.760
You know, like there is a sense of like you're never really out of it unless you're like a full lap box.

00:13:43.840 --> 00:13:46.860
Yeah. You know, if you can see someone, you can catch them.

00:13:46.940 --> 00:13:51.240
They're never going like, oh, but he's going 200 miles an hour and I'm only going 100.

00:13:51.480 --> 00:13:53.600
You know, like no one's going that fast.

00:13:53.600 --> 00:14:05.600
And so I think, Sean, I couldn't find any developer interviews, but I do believe that the green and blue cars are specifically tuned to be the easier cars to race against.

00:14:05.600 --> 00:14:14.760
And that the yellow car is dependent on some variable factors, but will either be incredibly slow or incredibly fast depending on your behavior.

00:14:14.920 --> 00:14:22.160
Interesting. So I want to bring something up and it's weird to say because I don't, I don't know if it was a glitch or what.

00:14:22.160 --> 00:14:23.560
This happened on my last race.

00:14:24.220 --> 00:14:27.460
I got lapped, but I was in third place.

00:14:27.960 --> 00:14:33.040
The other two cars were very near me and the first place was just flying.

00:14:33.360 --> 00:14:38.380
I didn't notice until after he passed me and my place didn't change.

00:14:38.600 --> 00:14:43.180
And then I watched the map and he was just flying like double, maybe triple the speed of anyone else.

00:14:43.180 --> 00:14:44.680
I can diagnose this for you.

00:14:44.680 --> 00:14:46.080
I was going to save this. I thought I was losing my mind.

00:14:46.100 --> 00:14:48.880
I was going to save this for later, but we can troubleshoot it for you here.

00:14:48.960 --> 00:14:50.900
Okay. Was that car the yellow car?

00:14:50.980 --> 00:14:54.020
I don't remember. It would have to be, but it probably was.

00:14:54.120 --> 00:14:55.240
But I just wanted to know if you knew.

00:14:55.520 --> 00:15:03.160
And then do you feel like you were shooting an excessive amount of missiles on that particular course?

00:15:03.420 --> 00:15:11.040
Probably, yes. The game is programmed the yellow car that if you use an excessive amount of projectile weaponry, not just the missiles.

00:15:11.400 --> 00:15:12.420
There's probably bombs. Yeah. If that counts.

00:15:12.440 --> 00:15:13.740
Because I was using a lot of bombs.

00:15:13.740 --> 00:15:24.340
Yep. If you use an excessive amount of missiles or bombs on one particular course, the yellow car will automatically accelerate to 127 miles per hour,

00:15:24.400 --> 00:15:29.920
which cannot be matched by a player, and you will lose to the first place.

00:15:29.940 --> 00:15:33.040
Wait. When you say on one particular course, you mean it can only happen on that course?

00:15:33.040 --> 00:15:36.180
You mean that it doesn't, like, raid you across multiple tracks?

00:15:36.400 --> 00:15:38.280
Okay. Like, hey, now you've done too many missiles.

00:15:38.540 --> 00:15:44.620
I'm glad you mentioned that because I'm – I was, like – I honestly thought I was going crazy because it was just the only time it happened.

00:15:44.620 --> 00:16:07.280
Without going too much into the weeds in this one point, I had another theory because I never – I wasn't getting lapped, but I was noticing that if I didn't see the cars in front of me, like, they were that far ahead or that far behind, that the game was just sort of simulating a car going around the circuit and there were no, like, obstacles for them.

00:16:07.280 --> 00:16:13.360
It was just, like, this is how fast the car would go around the circuit, and I was, like, so that's why they're catching up or that's why they keep going faster.

00:16:13.600 --> 00:16:15.120
So maybe that explains it.

00:16:15.400 --> 00:16:17.020
Yeah, that's a fascinating thing.

00:16:17.140 --> 00:16:22.640
I'd love to know that too of, like, how it actually figures out where the computers would be.

00:16:22.760 --> 00:16:24.280
I imagine it's all just timed.

00:16:25.300 --> 00:16:34.220
Yeah. But if they are, like, in relation to how you're performing, like, that certain cars will slow down if you're being slow and certain cars will catch up if you're being fast,

00:16:34.220 --> 00:16:40.440
it's kind of weird to think of that being matched to a specific time, but maybe it's just matched to your time on the track.

00:16:40.580 --> 00:16:45.540
I wonder. Now I'm really curious as to whether or not they account for, like, each other shooting at each other.

00:16:45.720 --> 00:16:48.920
Yeah, I couldn't – I would be surprised if they did.

00:16:49.380 --> 00:16:50.420
Yeah, yeah, same.

00:16:50.780 --> 00:16:57.380
And the game ends when you finish in fourth place on any particular track, but you do get two continues,

00:16:57.380 --> 00:17:00.540
so you don't immediately have to start back from the very beginning.

00:17:00.900 --> 00:17:03.480
Did anybody get through every single track?

00:17:03.480 --> 00:17:06.060
I got – so you know how you can spell out Nintendo?

00:17:06.460 --> 00:17:09.260
Yep. I got to the last D, and then I lost.

00:17:09.520 --> 00:17:11.040
Oh, okay. The only D.

00:17:12.180 --> 00:17:17.240
I think that repeats after you complete the word.

00:17:17.440 --> 00:17:18.900
He meant the only D in Nintendo.

00:17:19.980 --> 00:17:21.360
Ah. I see.

00:17:22.680 --> 00:17:24.260
You're right. Yeah.

00:17:24.640 --> 00:17:26.540
Yeah, I didn't get through the entire course.

00:17:26.540 --> 00:17:28.620
I didn't find that they were getting harder either.

00:17:28.620 --> 00:17:30.800
I just think I wasn't getting any better.

00:17:30.820 --> 00:17:32.520
I found they were getting harder.

00:17:32.520 --> 00:17:35.180
They started to add the walls that would pop up and that kind of thing.

00:17:35.460 --> 00:17:37.920
Okay. Yeah. And, yeah.

00:17:38.260 --> 00:17:40.360
But – I guess I meant the AI itself.

00:17:40.620 --> 00:17:42.080
Oh, the AI itself. Like, that they were getting better drivers.

00:17:42.200 --> 00:17:45.760
Did you think they were becoming better drivers? No, I think the courses would just change and they would trip me up.

00:17:45.760 --> 00:17:51.540
Between how many obstacles and different kinds of obstacles that they would lay out and they

00:17:51.540 --> 00:17:59.180
sort of had – before each race, they would give you sort of a rating on how rough the

00:17:59.180 --> 00:18:05.060
terrain was. I think they were just depending on those variables and determining how difficult the track was

00:18:05.060 --> 00:18:06.980
going to be. I don't think the AI changed at all.

00:18:07.200 --> 00:18:08.260
Got it. Yeah. And that makes sense.

00:18:08.520 --> 00:18:09.620
Yeah. All right.

00:18:09.780 --> 00:18:17.520
So, obstacles-wise, you have some puddles, some oil slicks, which it's a different fill

00:18:17.520 --> 00:18:20.300
color, but sometimes it's hard to tell the difference.

00:18:21.260 --> 00:18:23.860
It's described in the manual as rain squalls.

00:18:24.640 --> 00:18:26.460
There's very localized storms.

00:18:26.460 --> 00:18:28.160
Yeah, very localized rain.

00:18:28.320 --> 00:18:31.040
Like, rain happening right above you in this particular cloud.

00:18:32.120 --> 00:18:36.060
The pop-up barriers that you were talking about, which are very difficult because they

00:18:36.060 --> 00:18:41.120
chose not to just be walls, but they chose to be walls that will just pop up whenever

00:18:41.120 --> 00:18:41.920
they see fit.

00:18:42.500 --> 00:18:46.920
I'm sure they're timed to something, but it seemed to be very convenient to when I was

00:18:46.920 --> 00:18:47.840
driving around them.

00:18:48.320 --> 00:18:50.980
Yeah. And then there's those skulls.

00:18:51.540 --> 00:18:53.440
I never really touched one of those.

00:18:53.440 --> 00:18:54.340
I thought I was supposed to pick those up.

00:18:54.480 --> 00:18:55.940
Yeah. What happens with the skulls?

00:18:55.940 --> 00:18:56.900
I didn't touch them.

00:18:57.220 --> 00:19:03.040
I don't actually know because I was still in the races that I picked them up in.

00:19:03.800 --> 00:19:06.680
Maybe they, like, cancel out, like...

00:19:06.680 --> 00:19:07.580
Oh, your power-ups?

00:19:07.620 --> 00:19:08.700
Maybe. Your tune-up items? Yeah.

00:19:08.820 --> 00:19:09.700
Maybe. Yeah.

00:19:10.240 --> 00:19:12.260
I wasn't paying enough attention, I guess.

00:19:12.380 --> 00:19:17.940
And I do like that it lists the other RC drones, the other, you know, computer players

00:19:17.940 --> 00:19:20.180
that you're playing against, as items to avoid.

00:19:20.380 --> 00:19:21.420
Like, don't touch them.

00:19:21.680 --> 00:19:23.860
Sometimes when your benefit is smashing them.

00:19:24.240 --> 00:19:28.660
Yeah. Yeah, I was kind of expecting, like, just regular cars to just join in the race and, like, go,

00:19:28.740 --> 00:19:30.520
don't touch these RC cars, they'll explode.

00:19:30.580 --> 00:19:34.600
I thought it was just going to be stationary cars that were just left out on the track.

00:19:34.960 --> 00:19:37.600
Yeah. And not all the items are bad in the game.

00:19:37.600 --> 00:19:39.320
You can also get some good stuff, too.

00:19:39.860 --> 00:19:41.600
There's the... They call them zippers.

00:19:41.800 --> 00:19:43.200
I just think of them as accelerators.

00:19:43.520 --> 00:19:44.800
Yeah. Zipper was just a funny...

00:19:44.800 --> 00:19:45.680
Like, that's already a thing.

00:19:45.880 --> 00:19:47.840
Like, we already used that word for something else.

00:19:47.840 --> 00:19:53.020
Zipper was just... And then there's the, like, line drawing of the roll cage.

00:19:53.580 --> 00:19:54.680
Ah. See, I never...

00:19:54.680 --> 00:19:58.040
I didn't know what that was until I looked in the manual after the fact.

00:19:58.080 --> 00:20:01.680
It's a good thing to have, too, because it protects you against the missile or the bomb,

00:20:01.680 --> 00:20:02.600
I think, like, once.

00:20:02.880 --> 00:20:05.760
Mm-hmm. And then there's the missiles themselves to pick up.

00:20:06.320 --> 00:20:08.700
And the bombs, which have a smiley face on them.

00:20:08.860 --> 00:20:09.760
Yeah. That's cute.

00:20:09.880 --> 00:20:10.860
Happy bombs. Bombs are fun. Yeah.

00:20:11.140 --> 00:20:13.880
And ammo, which is just a star.

00:20:14.180 --> 00:20:16.800
So, apparently, that's what ammo looks like in this universe.

00:20:17.420 --> 00:20:18.500
How did we...

00:20:18.500 --> 00:20:20.280
Did you guys think, like...

00:20:20.280 --> 00:20:23.740
I could have used more, personally, of these things on the course themselves.

00:20:23.900 --> 00:20:26.220
Not more variety, just more on the course.

00:20:26.440 --> 00:20:27.660
Which is the one that makes you glow?

00:20:28.200 --> 00:20:29.120
That's the roll cage.

00:20:29.300 --> 00:20:33.300
Yeah. That's the roll cage. Yeah, like, invulnerability from the missile.

00:20:33.320 --> 00:20:36.820
Yeah, but if you run into someone with that, it also messes them up, right?

00:20:37.240 --> 00:20:38.720
But they're track items to avoid.

00:20:38.760 --> 00:20:42.980
How could that be? No. Because I was hit by flashing guys, and I started...

00:20:42.980 --> 00:20:45.260
I think if you just get hit by anything in this game, you're screwed.

00:20:45.400 --> 00:20:46.900
Okay. I wasn't sure if that was the item or not.

00:20:47.040 --> 00:20:52.220
But then there's also this whole, like, turbo, hotter engine, and sticky tires thing.

00:20:52.640 --> 00:20:54.520
That, like... Yeah, I mean, that's what they call them.

00:20:54.640 --> 00:20:56.520
I'm not... I'm not saying that you should call it as...

00:20:56.520 --> 00:20:58.460
These are the things you see between the races, right?

00:20:58.460 --> 00:21:00.300
Yes. Between the races for your...

00:21:00.300 --> 00:21:02.000
Like, when it shows your track conditions.

00:21:02.260 --> 00:21:03.720
And it's, like, fair. Mm-hmm.

00:21:03.740 --> 00:21:08.860
And it's, like, all right. Yeah, I was referencing that before, and I guess I could kind of notice when...

00:21:08.860 --> 00:21:11.560
The track had better traction.

00:21:12.300 --> 00:21:14.800
Um, but other than that, I wasn't...

00:21:14.800 --> 00:21:17.480
I didn't feel as much of a variation in that.

00:21:17.580 --> 00:21:24.520
Yeah, apparently the turbo is for acceleration, while hotter engine is for, like, overall speed.

00:21:24.780 --> 00:21:26.400
Top speed. Yeah. Yeah, top speed.

00:21:26.400 --> 00:21:29.080
I don't think that's how you measure that in real life.

00:21:29.480 --> 00:21:31.840
The hotter the engine, the top or the speed.

00:21:32.100 --> 00:21:34.800
I think the hotter the engine, like, the worse the car.

00:21:34.980 --> 00:21:35.880
That's because we don't have zippers.

00:21:36.160 --> 00:21:37.040
Yeah. That's why we...

00:21:37.040 --> 00:21:38.120
Yeah, yeah, yeah. It just needs some zippers.

00:21:38.340 --> 00:21:44.340
Also, there's no real difference in life between, like, top speed and acceleration, too.

00:21:44.540 --> 00:21:46.640
Like, I guess how fast you can get from zero to 60.

00:21:46.920 --> 00:21:48.540
Yeah, there is a... No, no, I know.

00:21:48.540 --> 00:21:58.840
But I'm saying, like, in this particular thing, the game's acceleration is only measured by, like, this treadmill that sends you off faster, you know, because it's running at a higher speed than your car is currently going.

00:21:59.060 --> 00:22:02.840
Okay. There's nothing like that in real life.

00:22:03.280 --> 00:22:05.340
The treadmill. I guess I don't follow.

00:22:05.400 --> 00:22:06.580
Yeah, I'm not sure. Yeah.

00:22:06.580 --> 00:22:16.320
The idea that, like, you know, oh, I'm going 100 and I can get to, like, 160 for, like, a brief moment in time and then back to my normal cruising top speed.

00:22:16.340 --> 00:22:18.480
Oh, is that what it means? Is that what turbo is talking about?

00:22:18.480 --> 00:22:20.340
That's what the turbo is for, the acceleration only.

00:22:20.340 --> 00:22:24.100
I thought it was acceleration as in, like, how quickly you get from zero to your top speed.

00:22:24.200 --> 00:22:28.960
That's what I understood. It's for the acceleration of the...

00:22:28.960 --> 00:22:30.380
The zippers? You might be great.

00:22:30.540 --> 00:22:32.000
I think it affects both, theoretically.

00:22:32.000 --> 00:22:37.040
Like, it affects your zipper because I noticed that your zip, you know, like, when you cross over a zipper, it goes faster.

00:22:37.240 --> 00:22:40.480
But, yeah, I guess it would also increase how quickly you get to your top speed.

00:22:40.660 --> 00:22:41.480
That is acceleration.

00:22:41.800 --> 00:22:42.620
Yeah, which is acceleration.

00:22:42.740 --> 00:22:47.060
So then there is a real life course for this and you've just watched Mike talk about it.

00:22:48.900 --> 00:22:51.460
It was worth going through the motions, though, for all you guys.

00:22:52.580 --> 00:22:55.300
And then we have to talk about those Nintendo letters.

00:22:55.480 --> 00:22:56.520
You brought them up real fast.

00:22:56.680 --> 00:22:58.720
But it's kind of a shame that you only got them to D.

00:22:59.060 --> 00:23:02.660
I know. I was so upset. Because there's a thing when you collect the Nintendo letters.

00:23:02.760 --> 00:23:05.320
There is a reason. Would you like to tell us why, Sam?

00:23:05.480 --> 00:23:06.960
I believe you get a new car.

00:23:07.080 --> 00:23:08.020
You get a new car!

00:23:08.660 --> 00:23:10.880
You had a good, like, announcer voice for that.

00:23:11.100 --> 00:23:14.660
Thank you. You start with what they call the standard truck.

00:23:15.000 --> 00:23:18.520
And then when you spell out Nintendo, you get the four-wheeler.

00:23:18.820 --> 00:23:23.500
And then if you manage to spell it out one more time, you get the off-roader, which is the fastest car.

00:23:23.500 --> 00:23:25.020
I definitely got that off-roader.

00:23:25.180 --> 00:23:27.920
Nice. And, yeah, that's a cool-looking car just in general, too.

00:23:27.920 --> 00:23:34.240
It is. See, I only got the four-wheeler, but do all the other cars get them, too?

00:23:34.240 --> 00:23:35.200
I don't know.

00:23:35.260 --> 00:23:37.060
I was kind of... I think that's what happened.

00:23:37.260 --> 00:23:41.160
And I was kind of like, well, why did I go through all that work if we're going to even out the playing field here?

00:23:42.440 --> 00:23:43.560
Well, let me put it this way.

00:23:43.580 --> 00:23:44.940
I never collected Nintendo either.

00:23:44.940 --> 00:23:53.080
But I can't imagine a world in which, when you have the off-roader, that it would be a fair fight against a bunch of standard trucks.

00:23:53.260 --> 00:23:54.980
Well, yeah, but then what's the point of getting it anywhere?

00:23:55.080 --> 00:23:56.400
What's the difference? Because it looks cool.

00:23:56.540 --> 00:23:59.840
It's harder. Maybe it's like you're in a new league now, you know?

00:24:00.240 --> 00:24:01.180
Yeah. And now everyone's faster.

00:24:01.360 --> 00:24:02.780
You've moved up to a new bracket.

00:24:02.940 --> 00:24:07.880
Or maybe they all coincidentally collected the Nintendo at the same pace as you.

00:24:08.140 --> 00:24:09.960
Sure. Maybe. Good idea. They were also collecting.

00:24:10.140 --> 00:24:11.480
It was a tag team system.

00:24:11.880 --> 00:24:13.080
You just happen to be the only one to collect.

00:24:13.180 --> 00:24:19.260
But it just doesn't seem like too much of a beneficial thing if everyone else is going to have it also.

00:24:19.560 --> 00:24:21.700
Yeah, but then if you just win every race easily...

00:24:21.700 --> 00:24:22.640
Well, yeah, I agree.

00:24:22.800 --> 00:24:23.680
Why is it in the game?

00:24:23.780 --> 00:24:24.860
Right. Either way.

00:24:25.040 --> 00:24:26.760
It's for more skilled players, too.

00:24:26.800 --> 00:24:30.260
Right. So you think it's harder to use these other...

00:24:30.260 --> 00:24:31.940
Well, if they are noticeably fast...

00:24:31.940 --> 00:24:33.300
I didn't get it, so I can't say it.

00:24:33.300 --> 00:24:39.320
But if it is faster than the regular car, then wouldn't the average speed of each course be faster?

00:24:39.540 --> 00:24:43.200
Sure. Yeah, I guess for that sense, to make it more of a challenge on yourself.

00:24:43.380 --> 00:24:45.740
When I read it in the manual, I thought it was like, oh, this is an upgrade.

00:24:45.740 --> 00:24:46.900
It's going to make me better.

00:24:47.100 --> 00:24:50.300
Joe just wants less features in everything he wants.

00:24:50.500 --> 00:24:52.860
That's not true. You've got to understand what I'm saying.

00:24:52.920 --> 00:24:57.360
I know. I totally get what you're saying, and it makes a lot more sense than my acceleration car.

00:24:57.440 --> 00:24:58.520
It's like...

00:24:59.380 --> 00:25:03.280
Just to put it out there, it's like if in Mario or CTR or this...

00:25:03.300 --> 00:25:07.320
You hit a speed boost, but everyone else boosts at the same time as you all boost.

00:25:07.340 --> 00:25:08.380
It's not the same.

00:25:08.460 --> 00:25:10.380
It's similar. Not the same.

00:25:35.980 --> 00:25:39.900
Not the same.

00:25:43.960 --> 00:25:48.760
Now, I couldn't find anything on development for RC Pro-Am.

00:25:48.920 --> 00:25:54.080
It is worth mentioning that Rare, the company that is now owned by Microsoft, that made Slalom,

00:25:54.120 --> 00:25:56.540
a game that we once had Essential, and...

00:25:56.540 --> 00:25:57.460
We don't talk about that.

00:25:57.600 --> 00:25:59.500
Yeah, and Sam's like, I have no idea.

00:25:59.500 --> 00:26:00.620
I've never played that game.

00:26:00.620 --> 00:26:01.980
I have an inaudible face right now.

00:26:02.060 --> 00:26:04.480
Yeah. And they also made Wizards and Warriors.

00:26:05.240 --> 00:26:08.760
Okay. So, you know, this is where we're at with them.

00:26:09.100 --> 00:26:10.300
And now we have this one.

00:26:10.620 --> 00:26:15.060
And I think we're missing one other that they released earlier in the same year that I can't

00:26:15.060 --> 00:26:17.920
think of. So, forget that I'm having this conversation.

00:26:18.240 --> 00:26:21.700
But anyway, what I meant to say was that usually it's pretty easy to find information about these

00:26:21.700 --> 00:26:23.540
things, but they don't want to talk about RC Pro-Am.

00:26:23.540 --> 00:26:27.640
So, instead, I was like, well, what are we going to talk about other than the Essential

00:26:27.640 --> 00:26:34.520
Games list? And I happened to stumble upon Nintendo Fun Club issue number five, where RC Pro-Am is

00:26:34.520 --> 00:26:35.420
the cover story.

00:26:35.560 --> 00:26:37.040
Ooh. That's a big deal.

00:26:37.240 --> 00:26:42.100
Like, you know, usually that's reserved for, like, Zelda or Metroid or Super Mario Brothers,

00:26:42.100 --> 00:26:49.740
but RC Pro-Am gets the cover story, and it's got a cute little, like, illustration of the,

00:26:49.740 --> 00:26:52.260
like, cart that you will never drive in the game.

00:26:52.300 --> 00:26:53.580
It doesn't even look like the off-roader.

00:26:53.820 --> 00:26:59.940
It looks like an actual, like, cart, you know, like, that you would own yourself.

00:27:00.200 --> 00:27:01.800
And I'm like, wow, that's not even in the game.

00:27:01.820 --> 00:27:10.580
That's confusing. But it's all worth it because the big cover story is RC Pro-Am.

00:27:10.580 --> 00:27:15.360
The big difference is RC, and we're not talking cola.

00:27:16.060 --> 00:27:20.620
What a quick way to out-date yourself right off the bat.

00:27:21.080 --> 00:27:24.180
RC Cola, which was delicious, if anybody's ever had some.

00:27:24.220 --> 00:27:28.120
Is that not around anymore? I don't think it's really, like, as common as you would find it back then.

00:27:28.260 --> 00:27:31.260
Like, you know, back then it was, like, up there with Pepsi and Coca-Cola.

00:27:31.440 --> 00:27:34.840
Now it's, like, good luck finding that outside of the Midwest, probably.

00:27:35.520 --> 00:27:37.300
But I like the taste of RC Cola.

00:27:37.760 --> 00:27:40.000
How about you, Sean? I wasn't a fan.

00:27:40.000 --> 00:27:40.900
Okay, interesting.

00:27:41.140 --> 00:27:42.320
Too flat? Yeah.

00:27:42.660 --> 00:27:43.900
Yeah, you need bubbles in your cola.

00:27:44.260 --> 00:27:45.840
I need the bubbles. Did you ever have any cola, RC?

00:27:45.840 --> 00:27:46.940
I've never had it. Okay.

00:27:47.880 --> 00:27:48.840
And you grew up?

00:27:49.220 --> 00:27:51.160
New Jersey. Yeah, they wouldn't have had it.

00:27:52.740 --> 00:27:58.020
But I'm not going to talk about RC Pro-Am in Fun Club Issue 5 because they don't cover

00:27:58.020 --> 00:27:59.640
anything different than what we've talked about.

00:27:59.880 --> 00:28:02.880
Instead, I want to talk about something in the coming soon section.

00:28:03.180 --> 00:28:07.720
Okay. In the coming soon section, there's a bunch of games that by now we've already talked about.

00:28:07.840 --> 00:28:10.560
And I'm like, oh, that's kind of weird that they're treating them like they're coming soon

00:28:10.560 --> 00:28:12.220
when we've played them, theoretically.

00:28:12.720 --> 00:28:14.760
However, there is one that caught my eye.

00:28:15.960 --> 00:28:18.980
Return of Donkey Kong for the NES.

00:28:18.980 --> 00:28:24.000
Yes. That barrel-throwing rascal, Donkey Kong, is some kind of crazy.

00:28:24.540 --> 00:28:27.320
In Donkey Kong, he has Pauline in his clutches.

00:28:27.580 --> 00:28:30.340
In Donkey Kong Jr., he gets locked in a cage.

00:28:30.620 --> 00:28:31.540
In Donkey Kong Jr.

00:28:31.680 --> 00:28:34.420
Math, he knows the score in a math jungle.

00:28:34.660 --> 00:28:36.080
That's like not really.

00:28:36.300 --> 00:28:37.780
That's not really what it is.

00:28:37.840 --> 00:28:38.740
He knows the score.

00:28:39.040 --> 00:28:40.960
At 3 a.m., the writer's like, I don't know.

00:28:41.180 --> 00:28:43.840
I don't know. There was a Donkey Kong Jr.

00:28:43.960 --> 00:28:47.760
Math. Yeah, he actually went to Miyamoto first, and he's like, is Donkey Kong Jr.

00:28:47.880 --> 00:28:53.600
Math canon? And in Donkey Kong 3, he makes trouble for Stanley the Bugman.

00:28:53.800 --> 00:28:59.540
And now, Donkey Kong is soon to come back with more barrel-tossing fun.

00:28:59.860 --> 00:29:02.140
But this time, you're in control.

00:29:02.600 --> 00:29:04.500
Are you video buff enough?

00:29:05.000 --> 00:29:06.060
What is that?

00:29:06.400 --> 00:29:11.240
Are you video buff enough to handle the one and only return of Donkey Kong?

00:29:11.720 --> 00:29:12.720
Watch for it.

00:29:13.100 --> 00:29:15.040
Maybe this is the only return.

00:29:15.600 --> 00:29:16.920
And it never happened.

00:29:16.940 --> 00:29:19.520
But also, what's weird about this, right?

00:29:19.880 --> 00:29:23.840
Is like, this is what I said I wanted in Donkey Kong 3.

00:29:24.020 --> 00:29:26.460
Remember? This was a me and Sean only episode.

00:29:26.640 --> 00:29:30.880
But I said that it seems natural that if in Donkey Kong 1, you're playing as Mario,

00:29:31.100 --> 00:29:34.940
and in Donkey Kong Jr., you're playing as Donkey Kong Jr.,

00:29:35.460 --> 00:29:38.340
that in Donkey Kong 3, you would finally want to be able to play as Donkey Kong,

00:29:38.440 --> 00:29:40.520
but instead you play as Stanley the Bugman.

00:29:41.940 --> 00:29:43.920
So this sounds like it would have been great.

00:29:43.940 --> 00:29:46.860
I don't really know what you would have done because it mentions barrel-tossing.

00:29:47.680 --> 00:29:49.760
I don't know. Would you have taken out Mario?

00:29:50.120 --> 00:29:51.520
Maybe you're trying to attack him.

00:29:51.660 --> 00:29:56.540
Do you think that maybe this is referencing the classics?

00:29:56.800 --> 00:30:01.540
Donkey Kong classics? So I thought about that, but it is an entirely different game

00:30:01.540 --> 00:30:06.500
because it doesn't say that you're, you know, when it says,

00:30:06.620 --> 00:30:08.200
but this time you're in control.

00:30:08.360 --> 00:30:09.260
Yeah. It sounds pretty new.

00:30:09.260 --> 00:30:10.340
It's referring to Donkey Kong.

00:30:10.520 --> 00:30:12.220
Like, oh, he's done all these crazy things,

00:30:12.240 --> 00:30:15.140
and now this time there's more barrel-tossing fun,

00:30:15.280 --> 00:30:17.120
but you're in control of the barrel-tossing.

00:30:17.300 --> 00:30:18.360
Does it say that?

00:30:18.480 --> 00:30:20.500
It says, you're in control.

00:30:20.500 --> 00:30:23.400
Now Donkey Kong is soon to come back with more barrel-tossing fun,

00:30:23.500 --> 00:30:24.700
but this time you're in control.

00:30:24.980 --> 00:30:26.200
I don't know. No, no, no.

00:30:26.240 --> 00:30:31.460
The but this time implies that you are not controlling Mario anymore

00:30:31.460 --> 00:30:32.800
or stand a bug freak.

00:30:33.040 --> 00:30:35.860
You heard the jungle math thing that he was saying before.

00:30:35.900 --> 00:30:37.180
I don't think this guy's a very good writer.

00:30:37.440 --> 00:30:39.880
But Donkey Kong Jr. Man is also not in Donkey Kong classics,

00:30:39.980 --> 00:30:41.180
so why bring that up?

00:30:41.300 --> 00:30:42.760
Yeah. You fool.

00:30:43.400 --> 00:30:49.960
That's a good point. Also, I found a stub on this on the Mario wiki about unreleased games,

00:30:50.060 --> 00:30:52.960
and apparently this was mentioned in one other place besides this,

00:30:53.200 --> 00:30:54.340
Return of Donkey Kong.

00:30:54.520 --> 00:30:55.900
I would have loved to see it.

00:30:56.340 --> 00:30:58.980
Who knows if it made it past this stub?

00:30:59.400 --> 00:31:01.420
Yeah. Someone just wrote,

00:31:01.560 --> 00:31:03.400
like, oh, we're going to make a Return of Donkey Kong game,

00:31:03.440 --> 00:31:04.600
and it never happened.

00:31:04.840 --> 00:31:08.020
But it's worth noting that sometimes when you read these older magazines,

00:31:08.040 --> 00:31:12.000
you can find some fun announced games that never actually happened.

00:31:12.520 --> 00:31:18.900
I know in Nintendo Power early on in the 64 time of that magazine,

00:31:19.180 --> 00:31:23.600
they announced, like, Earthbound 64 and, like, had images of it and everything.

00:31:23.800 --> 00:31:25.160
Were these April issues?

00:31:25.520 --> 00:31:26.340
No, this was real.

00:31:26.340 --> 00:31:30.760
And, like, it was shown at, like, a tech demo in Space World or something like that,

00:31:30.820 --> 00:31:32.000
which was Nintendo's E3.

00:31:32.600 --> 00:31:36.220
But, like, it's just funny to see, like, when that thing gets to a magazine,

00:31:36.360 --> 00:31:39.820
that means it's, like, it's coming closer to being a real deal.

00:31:39.820 --> 00:31:44.740
And everybody was waiting for Mother 64, Earthbound 64, I guess they called it.

00:31:45.460 --> 00:31:46.400
And it never happened.

00:31:46.580 --> 00:31:48.460
So I think it's just funny when you spot things like this.

00:31:48.760 --> 00:31:52.600
According to Sean, does any of this really matter if they don't come out?

00:31:52.600 --> 00:31:56.680
That was a thing that he called out in the Best of 87 episode when I was like,

00:31:56.740 --> 00:32:00.820
this game said that it was coming out in 87, but it won't come out until January of 88.

00:32:01.100 --> 00:32:04.660
And you're like, is it breaking news that a game has been delayed in the past?

00:32:04.900 --> 00:32:10.480
I remember that. So if you didn't listen to the Best of 87 episode, go back just for that moment.

00:32:10.600 --> 00:32:20.920
It's really fun. I mean, changing the subject, though, I'm very, I'm confused how this game has literally no trivia about it.

00:32:21.020 --> 00:32:27.500
Like, this seems like it was a big enough game to have at least some behind-the-scenes of the developer

00:32:27.500 --> 00:32:36.300
or some weird happenstance aside from just its appearance in the same magazine issue of Vaporware.

00:32:36.520 --> 00:32:38.480
Right, right. Essentially, yeah.

00:32:38.600 --> 00:32:43.160
And that's what's so strange about it, too, is like, you know, if you look at it,

00:32:43.300 --> 00:32:45.840
it does get a sequel, too, that we'll talk about in a minute.

00:32:46.040 --> 00:32:51.360
But, like, there's a development stub on the wiki as well, and it just says a bunch of nothing.

00:32:51.360 --> 00:32:58.180
It's like, the game was originally titled Pro-Am Racing, but then was changed to RC Pro-Am when they added the remote control.

00:32:58.340 --> 00:32:59.360
That is interesting.

00:33:00.920 --> 00:33:06.600
I guess that is interesting, because now we know maybe that they decided, like,

00:33:06.620 --> 00:33:09.880
wow, this doesn't really feel like you're driving this car because of the top-down angle.

00:33:10.140 --> 00:33:12.360
So then they were like, let's just add RC in there.

00:33:12.820 --> 00:33:14.160
Yeah. I'm fascinated.

00:33:15.360 --> 00:33:18.040
Yeah. So I don't care what you guys say.

00:33:18.480 --> 00:33:22.740
Yeah, and I don't consider things like, you know, we've talked about David Weiss so many times,

00:33:22.760 --> 00:33:24.940
because he's always the composer on these Rare games.

00:33:24.960 --> 00:33:29.780
I don't consider, like, things from him tidbits anymore, because it's like, he's just talking about the music.

00:33:30.000 --> 00:33:36.180
And to me, RC Pro-Am wasn't like, the music wasn't as good as, like, say, what Wizards and Warriors was bringing.

00:33:36.420 --> 00:33:37.600
It was still okay.

00:33:37.820 --> 00:33:39.980
It was possible. I think that's not worth talking.

00:33:40.060 --> 00:33:43.520
The thing is, though, with this, there's lots of sound effects that are happening at the same time.

00:33:43.540 --> 00:33:46.480
There's the engine revving, and all those take up the music tracks.

00:33:46.680 --> 00:33:48.460
Right. You know, the soundtracks.

00:33:48.740 --> 00:33:52.880
Yeah, and I did notice, I didn't notice until late into playing through it, that you also hear the,

00:33:53.400 --> 00:33:57.340
when a car is coming close to entering your screen, you hear their engine.

00:33:57.400 --> 00:33:58.680
You hear them, voo-oo-oo.

00:33:59.180 --> 00:34:00.060
You know, whatever you call that.

00:34:00.100 --> 00:34:01.020
I never noticed. Yeah.

00:34:01.280 --> 00:34:02.840
But there's that. There's the skidding sound.

00:34:02.980 --> 00:34:04.180
There's a bunch of sounds that are happening.

00:34:04.400 --> 00:34:06.060
Well, good sound design shouldn't be noticed.

00:34:06.920 --> 00:34:08.660
Whoa. Look at this guy.

00:34:09.220 --> 00:34:10.080
Who is this guy?

00:34:10.460 --> 00:34:12.220
Yeah, who was that? I don't know.

00:34:12.640 --> 00:34:23.800
He left. Anyway, we have to talk about the sequels and spinoffs, because that's apparently a thing we talk about in every episode when we have them.

00:34:23.880 --> 00:34:24.720
And this time we have them.

00:34:24.720 --> 00:34:28.060
There was a Genesis version of RC Pro-Am.

00:34:28.220 --> 00:34:30.920
It was called Championship Pro-Am.

00:34:32.000 --> 00:34:35.000
It's basically the same game as the NES version.

00:34:35.220 --> 00:34:37.960
However, there's five opponents instead of three.

00:34:38.980 --> 00:34:41.720
You spell out Champion instead of Nintendo.

00:34:43.080 --> 00:34:46.360
And obviously, like the Genesis being 16-bit, it looks better.

00:34:47.200 --> 00:34:53.140
Did they get rid of the little antenna that told you that they were RC cars?

00:34:53.420 --> 00:34:54.620
You know, that I don't know.

00:34:54.880 --> 00:35:02.680
I can look that up while we talk about the next one, but it would imply that if you're losing the RC part, you've got to get rid of that.

00:35:02.680 --> 00:35:09.240
However, I'm looking at it right now, and to be honest, it doesn't look – yeah, the antennas are still there, and the graphics aren't that much better.

00:35:10.160 --> 00:35:13.340
You know, like for what it's worth, I don't think that that's all that different.

00:35:14.080 --> 00:35:14.920
Right? Yeah.

00:35:15.140 --> 00:35:16.460
Maybe a little bit, but not much.

00:35:16.480 --> 00:35:20.560
No, I mean, it's nicer, but not worth a new purchase.

00:35:21.000 --> 00:35:22.180
You know, like, oh, I got a –

00:35:22.180 --> 00:35:23.000
Yeah. Oh, definitely.

00:35:23.120 --> 00:35:25.140
It's the remastered version of RC Pro-Am.

00:35:25.180 --> 00:35:27.160
I need it now. It's not like that.

00:35:27.160 --> 00:35:33.800
Yeah, and then there was a Game Boy version of RC Pro-Am called Super RC Pro-Am.

00:35:34.020 --> 00:35:35.540
Nothing super about it, though.

00:35:35.640 --> 00:35:36.840
It's black and white.

00:35:36.840 --> 00:35:38.320
It's really, really basic.

00:35:38.820 --> 00:35:46.500
It's pretty much the same game as the RC Pro-Am, but with less detail and portable.

00:35:47.220 --> 00:35:53.360
Nice. So I guess, like, you know, the portable part's nice, but I'm not like – I wasn't dying to play this game on the go.

00:35:53.500 --> 00:36:04.000
Yeah. Me neither. And RC Pro-Am 2, Roman numeral 2, it's always important, comes on the NES in 1992, so we'll be playing that.

00:36:04.400 --> 00:36:08.540
The major difference, the only one we're going to talk about for now because we'll cover it in that episode,

00:36:08.740 --> 00:36:13.300
is that it introduces four-player local multiplayer.

00:36:13.780 --> 00:36:15.960
Wow. The multi-tap.

00:36:15.960 --> 00:36:20.920
Finally, yeah, finally, you can play a game with three of your best friends on one NES.

00:36:20.920 --> 00:36:22.340
I'm curious how that works.

00:36:22.820 --> 00:36:24.940
Well, like what Sean just said, the multi-tap, essentially.

00:36:25.180 --> 00:36:27.080
But, like, yes, how does it – how do you see it all?

00:36:27.120 --> 00:36:28.680
That's what I mean. Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:36:28.880 --> 00:36:31.000
I thought you were talking about, like, how do you plug all those controllers in?

00:36:31.100 --> 00:36:32.520
There's only two slots. Split screen.

00:36:32.760 --> 00:36:36.860
Four-way split screen. Well, I mean, because the thing is, if you go off the screen, then you die.

00:36:37.520 --> 00:36:41.920
Well, I have played games that are top-down racing like that, where if you go off the screen, you do die.

00:36:41.980 --> 00:36:42.820
Oh, I've never played that game.

00:36:42.820 --> 00:36:44.540
And then you respawn near the middle.

00:36:44.960 --> 00:36:50.200
Oh, wow. Well, stay tuned for episode 643, RC Pro-Am 2.

00:36:50.820 --> 00:36:56.380
And in the meantime, let's cover the rest of this game in our Essential Games list.

00:36:59.160 --> 00:37:03.140
Sam, which way are you driving this list?

00:37:03.360 --> 00:37:05.180
Every way, because it's top-down.

00:37:06.240 --> 00:37:07.320
Sorry, that was dumb.

00:37:07.580 --> 00:37:10.980
It's fine. I'm going to vote this one Essential.

00:37:11.220 --> 00:37:15.660
I think that we haven't seen a racing game for this system yet.

00:37:15.880 --> 00:37:20.900
And I think that racing games are a huge and very important genre in video games.

00:37:21.060 --> 00:37:22.340
And I had a lot of fun with this one.

00:37:22.480 --> 00:37:24.060
I think that the controls are tight.

00:37:24.220 --> 00:37:26.880
It introduces a lot of different mechanics.

00:37:27.120 --> 00:37:31.920
There is – you know, you can fire weapons at each other.

00:37:32.340 --> 00:37:34.160
You can protect yourself.

00:37:34.340 --> 00:37:37.960
You can do all the – there's a lot of different variation to it.

00:37:37.960 --> 00:37:39.860
And I think it's really fun.

00:37:40.300 --> 00:37:44.280
So until RC Pro-Am 2 comes out, I'm going to say that –

00:37:44.280 --> 00:37:45.360
Assuming it's better.

00:37:45.620 --> 00:37:46.480
Yeah, assuming it's better.

00:37:47.740 --> 00:37:49.020
I'm going to vote Essential.

00:37:49.560 --> 00:37:50.740
All right. Sean?

00:37:51.600 --> 00:37:54.260
I'm actually with Sam on this.

00:37:54.960 --> 00:37:57.680
I've had way too much fun with this game.

00:37:57.680 --> 00:38:02.000
I know that I have my little nitpicks and gripes about its perspective.

00:38:02.000 --> 00:38:08.580
But I think that it was a breath of fresh air for the system and for just racing games in general.

00:38:08.740 --> 00:38:13.440
Because while it is an old concept, I still hadn't played a game like it in ages.

00:38:13.760 --> 00:38:22.160
It reminded me of a time – it was at a party that it was my stepdad's company through.

00:38:22.160 --> 00:38:27.520
And they had an RC car circuit brought in for the kids to play.

00:38:27.560 --> 00:38:29.460
And it felt exactly like that to me.

00:38:29.800 --> 00:38:31.880
So I had a lot of fun with this game.

00:38:31.880 --> 00:38:36.660
I think that it's unique enough that it deserves a spot on this list at this point.

00:38:36.800 --> 00:38:40.100
Yeah. In our playthrough of these games.

00:38:40.280 --> 00:38:41.700
So, yes, I'm voting in Essential.

00:38:42.100 --> 00:38:47.560
All right. Now, it's important to note that in the old rules, that would have made it an Essential game.

00:38:47.800 --> 00:38:54.720
No longer. But we have all voted that following our best of 1987, that you now need majority rule.

00:38:54.980 --> 00:39:01.140
So me or Joe must vote it into the Essential games as well for it to be an official Essential game.

00:39:01.400 --> 00:39:07.900
Joe? So there definitely was a time where I would have 100% voted this Essential.

00:39:07.900 --> 00:39:15.360
Especially because – I know we said they weren't racing games, but like Mock Rider and Excitebike and Slalom even.

00:39:15.360 --> 00:39:16.660
I voted Essential.

00:39:17.280 --> 00:39:19.500
But – oh, actually, I didn't vote Mock Rider, but you know what I'm saying.

00:39:20.740 --> 00:39:24.920
But now looking at it – I do think this is a better game than all three of those games.

00:39:24.940 --> 00:39:26.580
I enjoyed this more than all three of those games.

00:39:26.660 --> 00:39:35.160
But I don't think in the context of today – I think what I'm really enjoying about it, what's fun to me about it is in the context of the NES.

00:39:35.160 --> 00:39:38.480
I'm like, wow, I can't believe they were doing this then because it's cool that they're doing it.

00:39:38.500 --> 00:39:43.800
But there were still too many – it didn't – it just felt a little too repetitive to me after a while.

00:39:43.880 --> 00:39:46.600
After a while, I got a little tired of it.

00:39:46.680 --> 00:39:55.660
And for transparency, I played this game weeks ago for the first time and then played it again last night to refresh myself.

00:39:55.720 --> 00:40:00.280
And as I was playing it last night, I just wasn't as – I just felt like I had done this before.

00:40:00.280 --> 00:40:01.920
I wasn't as interested in going back.

00:40:02.300 --> 00:40:09.260
That being said, this I think is another one of my – I think I said this on the last episode, but another one of my – no, earlier episode.

00:40:09.320 --> 00:40:11.420
But another one of my higher play-its.

00:40:11.620 --> 00:40:14.960
It comes very close, but I'm just not going to throw it a vote.

00:40:15.660 --> 00:40:29.300
All right. I think that RC Pro-Am surprised me out the gate with like how willing I was to like play ball with like being bad at the game and keep trying.

00:40:29.600 --> 00:40:38.700
Sometimes when you're bad, especially like in side-scrolling action-adventure games on the NES, sometimes you're just like, oh, well, this is just NES hard and I'm not really going to try my way through it.

00:40:38.840 --> 00:40:44.100
There's something about RC Pro-Am that felt like it's like, oh, no, you should give this the time to learn it and figure it out.

00:40:44.100 --> 00:40:50.020
And so I did and I think that while I didn't become like a great player at RC Pro-Am, I enjoyed my time with it a lot.

00:40:50.600 --> 00:40:57.780
In terms of voting in an essential game, I think it's missing a few things to get me to also go over the edge.

00:40:58.000 --> 00:41:06.520
Most importantly in like the idea of what an essential game is, for a racing game, it needs to have some kind of multiplayer to still hold up today.

00:41:06.520 --> 00:41:16.700
I think playing against the actual like computer bots, it's not that they, I mean, they have great AI compared to things that we've played against, but it's not all that interesting.

00:41:16.880 --> 00:41:22.260
They do seem to just kind of like, if you're in their vicinity and they have a weapon, they'll attack you.

00:41:22.380 --> 00:41:24.820
Otherwise, for the most part, everybody's just driving around this course.

00:41:25.560 --> 00:41:36.080
The courses themselves, I would have liked to see some variety in like scenic differences other than just like this one is this kind of wavy and the other one is this kind of wavy.

00:41:36.280 --> 00:41:39.020
You know, like they're just like bent around different parts of the track.

00:41:39.220 --> 00:41:43.300
You know, it would have even been cool to just like do the alphabet or something like that.

00:41:43.300 --> 00:41:51.580
You know, like I don't know, like, you know, the style of the way the track is, you know, like this one's shaped like an L and this one's shaped like an N.

00:41:51.680 --> 00:41:52.820
And I don't know how you would do that.

00:41:53.040 --> 00:41:55.520
They have to start an N somewhere different.

00:41:56.280 --> 00:42:05.060
But it just seemed to be like all sorts of different bend out like turns of how fast you can react in later stages just added more obstacles to make things harder.

00:42:05.060 --> 00:42:12.200
As opposed to like challenging your ability to beat the other computers, which is what I always think like makes a fun racing game.

00:42:12.860 --> 00:42:18.320
I think like for me, an easy essential games thing is the Mario Kart series.

00:42:18.620 --> 00:42:21.520
Like what makes those so fun and I'm not saying like why isn't RC Pro?

00:42:21.660 --> 00:42:31.840
I'm like Mario Kart. What makes those so fun is like the challenge against everybody else and where you fall in like this giant 12 player ranking system where like, oh, you finish second.

00:42:31.840 --> 00:42:36.740
So this in this particular one, you go up 10 points, but first place went up 16.

00:42:36.920 --> 00:42:39.660
So it's like, oh, shit. Like, how am I going to catch up in this one?

00:42:39.760 --> 00:42:42.500
It's like just don't finish and last and, you know, keep playing.

00:42:42.860 --> 00:42:48.820
And I'm like, oh, I would have liked, you know, some kind of overall point or GP system in RC Pro Am.

00:42:50.240 --> 00:42:56.280
That said, I don't want anyone to think that like because I didn't adapt well to the controls that I'm that this is an essential game.

00:42:56.360 --> 00:42:58.080
I think like it's for the other reasons.

00:42:58.080 --> 00:43:05.820
Me not to do the controls was just like my head space not being able to like understand how to control in an isometric layout.

00:43:06.060 --> 00:43:07.720
Like that just wasn't working out well for me.

00:43:07.860 --> 00:43:10.160
I wasn't doing the weird turn thing.

00:43:10.240 --> 00:43:18.560
Whoever said that where like, you know, I think it was you, Joe, where you were talking about like when you turn and you start to think about like going.

00:43:18.680 --> 00:43:20.660
Screen left is not. Yeah. And you go backwards instead.

00:43:21.120 --> 00:43:22.500
That didn't happen to me.

00:43:22.640 --> 00:43:24.740
It just at first. It takes a while to get the hang of it.

00:43:24.740 --> 00:43:27.580
But I think I just had to overall like to better master the turns.

00:43:27.720 --> 00:43:31.480
I just had to play slower and it wound up making for a less enjoyable experience.

00:43:32.840 --> 00:43:34.320
Wow. So it's not essential.

00:43:35.240 --> 00:43:37.400
RC Pro Am blew a 2-0 lead.

00:43:37.720 --> 00:43:39.880
Yeah. Just a tie 2-2.

00:43:40.280 --> 00:43:43.300
Which is great. I think RC Pro Am should be proud of itself.

00:43:43.520 --> 00:43:47.160
But I think like, you know, 1988 is shaping up to be a great year.

00:43:47.160 --> 00:43:51.100
Sure. We've already had some interesting ones with Contra being essential.

00:43:51.580 --> 00:43:55.780
I think Gunsmoke was the one you were referring to being like a game that you were like, oh, it's so close.

00:43:55.780 --> 00:43:56.920
That was a while ago. Yeah.

00:43:57.080 --> 00:43:58.600
That was one you I think you were talking about though.

00:43:58.680 --> 00:44:00.520
Yeah, it was. Because I just listened to that episode.

00:44:00.740 --> 00:44:02.940
Right, right, right. So there's that.

00:44:03.460 --> 00:44:06.680
We're not getting, you know, like granted Karnov wasn't great.

00:44:06.680 --> 00:44:12.020
But I have nothing against like games like Renegade and Ice Hockey and.

00:44:12.260 --> 00:44:14.700
We should have a nothing against category.

00:44:15.420 --> 00:44:17.960
Town and Country Surf Designs Wood and Water Rage though.

00:44:18.260 --> 00:44:20.180
I do have something against that game.

00:44:21.400 --> 00:44:25.700
I think I called, I forgot, I forgot, I forgot, I already.

00:44:25.780 --> 00:44:27.440
